# Build brush snapping indicator

Source proposal: [BuildBrushSnappingIndicator.proposal.md](BuildBrushSnappingIndicator.proposal.md).
Status: Phases 1-5 complete, including target-mesh and texture-independent coverage corrections.
Phase 4 installed-game verification was confirmed and approved by the user on
2026-10-29. Phase 5 regression/distributable verification, second review, and
independent completion audit are complete. Final qualification in Phase 6 remains pending.
Format: Indented unchecked tasks follow the existing src\docs\project.todo convention.

## Goal and governing constraints

Deliver a client-owned, mesh-projected directional overlay on the original targeted
world block, not on the resolved placement preview. Communicate resolved
tangential snapping using the existing face-coordinate and world-axis rules:
cyan for valid placement and red for invalid placement. This user-directed
clarification supersedes the earlier suppression of invalid snapping positions.
Applied directions and placement validation semantics remain unchanged.

The approved proposal remains authoritative. This plan does not authorize implementation.
Preserve snapping thresholds, selection raycasting, placement destinations, native
validation behavior, preview synchronization, and network packets. Add no controls
or input capture, no authored indicator texture per block, and no server renderer
ownership of the overlay.

Non-goals: arbitrary mesh-edge discovery, shape-boundary snapping, universal custom
renderer support, and a face-normal glyph. W-only/ordinary fallback,
None, and outcomes with no permitted U/V movement show no tangential marking.

## Assumptions and decisions still requiring evidence

- Exact glyphs, width, opacity, and any combined corner cue are not approved
  artwork. Bands or chevrons are candidates for user evaluation in Phase 6.
  Validity colors are user-directed: valid cyan, invalid red.
- The user's Phase 4 correction replaces category-based default-mesh qualification
  with acquisition from actual native terrain tessellation. Do not reject block
  classes, behaviors, snow, variants, block entities, or draw types before acquisition.
  Evaluate cubes and non-cubic shapes using their captured positions/triangles.
- Native tessellation resolves world-instance geometry and transforms. Missing
  terrain output and GPU-only renderer/material deformation remain concrete
  acquisition/fidelity limitations, not a class/behavior whitelist.
  Texture holes/material alpha do not clip or disqualify the indicator: it
  intentionally covers the full eligible mesh, without bitmap scans or alpha
  sampling. Missing geometry is not replaced with a cube.
- Exact cache identity, world-transform acquisition, surface-facing data,
  render stage/order, and depth bias require investigation and evidence before
  their dependent behavior is accepted. These are implementation decisions, not
  permission to claim unverified visual fidelity.
- Component names below identify existing integration points or responsibilities;
  final names for new types should follow repository conventions.
- Use the existing .NET 10/xUnit project and installed game references through
  VINTAGE_STORY. Do not add a test framework or dependencies by default.
- Automated tests and C# builds cannot qualify appearance, overlay ordering,
  non-cubic readability, or rendering cost. User-run in-game checks remain required.
  The proposal sets no numeric performance budget; record measured cost and obtain
  an explicit acceptance decision rather than inventing a threshold.

## Phase 1: Applied outcome reporting and presentation contract

Depends on: No implementation phase; confirm implementation authorization first.
Outcome: Local placement resolution supplies a coherent, testable presentation
contract without changing placement or networking. No rendering is required yet.

    ✔ Confirm implementation authorization and inspect existing integration seams.
        ✔ Trace BrushSnapping, BrushPlacementValidation, BuildBrushInstance,
          BuildBrushControllerClient, and BuildBrushSystem_Client.
        ✔ Identify where the original world selection is available before its
          position becomes the placement destination, and where the final local
          placement outcome is known.
        ✔ Reuse existing result/mapping helpers where possible; record the
          chosen local result and presentation ownership in implementation documentation.
    ✔ Extend local outcome reporting in the placement-resolution path.
        ✔ Distinguish requested snapping, accepted snapping, ordinary fallback,
          and invalid placement even when their candidate positions coincide.
        ✔ Preserve U/V projection around the block-cell center and the existing
          0.15 threshold, with ordinary local boundaries at 0.35 and 0.65.
        ✔ Preserve horizontal X/Z and vertical Y filtering, both horizontal
          in-plane axes on top/bottom faces, restricted W fallback, independent
          explicit face offset without doubling W, and None face-adjacent placement.
        ✔ Keep rejected candidates invalid without an unrestricted retry.
          Presentation must not invoke native validation a second time.
    ✔ Define a coherent local presentation snapshot and visibility decision.
        ✔ Pair original target identity and face with their corresponding
          resolved outcome; include enough identity/version information to reject
          stale face, hit-region, snapping-mode, held-item, or target results.
        ✔ Carry each resolved in-plane direction independently; do not collapse
          top/bottom U and V into one value merely because both are horizontal.
        ✔ Require local build mode, an eligible held block, a current target,
          supported geometry, and resolved permitted tangential movement.
        ✔ Suppress centered/disabled-axis markings, None, W-only/ordinary fallback,
          and results not yet matched to the current target. Invalid snapping
          remains visible in red; accepted snapping remains cyan.
    ✔ Add focused tests in VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests.
        ✔ Extend BrushSnappingTests/BrushSnappingRestrictionTests as appropriate
          for all six faces, opposite-face signs, each snapping mode, and both
          top/bottom horizontal directions.
        ✔ Exercise values just below, exactly at, and just above both thresholds;
          assert existing boundary behavior rather than choosing new inclusivity.
        ✔ Cover explicit face offset with and without U/V or existing W movement,
          equal-position outcomes with different meanings, and invalid candidates.
        ✔ Verify presentation does not add native validation calls, and mismatched
          or newly observed targets/faces cannot reuse an old successful outcome.

Completion criteria:
    ✔ Focused tests pass and demonstrate unchanged placement results and validation
      behavior, plus coherent presentation data independent of renderer/network changes.

Verification: Delegated focused VSTest run rebuilt the mod and tests using .NET SDK
10.0.401 and VINTAGE_STORY=G:\Vintagestory: 1,734 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped.
The second implementation review and independent audit-stage-completion review
both found Phase 1 fully satisfied. The local contract and ownership are documented
in [BuildBrushArchitecture.md](BuildBrushArchitecture.md#local-snapping-outcome-and-presentation-contract).
No geometry, shader, rendering, packaging, or live visual/performance qualification
is claimed by this phase.

## Phase 2: Supported target geometry and owned cache

Depends on: Phase 1 presentation contract.
Outcome: Supported original targets yield reproducible mesh/transform data;
unsupported targets are explicitly omitted. This does not yet qualify visual quality.

    ✔ Establish target-mesh acquisition and an initial support decision.
        ✔ Investigate ITesselatorManager.GetDefaultBlockMesh as a static-geometry
          starting point; verify selected-world-instance mesh and transform fidelity.
        ✔ Do not treat GetDefaultBlockMeshRef inventory geometry or
          MiniDimensionTessellator preview output as proof of world-target fidelity.
        ✔ Identify candidate cubes and non-cubic static shapes for qualification,
          including narrow/recessed surfaces that may not intersect a procedural mask.
        ✔ Explicitly reject unqualified geometry categories listed in the assumptions.
          Texture holes and material alpha must not restrict mesh eligibility or
          require bitmap analysis/alpha sampling; markings intentionally cover them.
        ✔ Omit geometry that cannot be reproduced accurately or carry meaningful
          markings. Never substitute a generic cube.
    ✔ Implement clearly separated target-mesh handling and cache ownership.
        ✔ Define geometry identity separately from target transform/presentation,
          including all qualified variant inputs needed to avoid incorrect reuse.
        ✔ Keep engine-cached mesh data borrowed; copy before mutation and dispose
          only overlay-owned resources.
        ✔ Reuse valid geometry for face, hit-region, and snapping-mode changes.
          Equivalent supported instances may reuse geometry and change only transform.
        ✔ Invalidate relevant resources on geometry/world/resource changes and
          shader reload; rebuild/upload only when required on the proper engine thread.
        ✔ Make owned resource replacement and disposal testable for later lifecycle
          wiring without introducing a general-purpose cache redesign.
    ✔ Add focused support/cache tests.
        ✔ Assert unsupported targets yield no overlay mesh rather than cube fallback.
        ✔ Assert repeated face/hit/mode changes do not retessellate; equivalent
          instance reuse updates the transform without stale target identity.
        ✔ Assert geometry/lifecycle invalidation cannot reuse obsolete data,
          borrowed meshes are unchanged/not disposed, and owned replacements are released.

Completion criteria:
    ✔ Support decisions, cache identity, transforms, and resource ownership are
      documented and covered by focused tests. Runtime fidelity/readability of
      candidate shapes remains explicitly pending Phase 6.

Corrected verification: The earlier cuboid-only and texture-alpha eligibility
decisions and completion reviews are superseded. Phase 2 was corrected to preserve general
faithful static target meshes and evaluate actual selected-facing triangle
opportunity, including slopes, multi-element shapes, and recessed surfaces.
The later live-rendering correction supersedes the CPU opportunity gate as well:
the shader alone performs selected-facing U/V union clipping on captured geometry.
Selection-box hits need not touch a rendered triangle or its outer bounds.
Delegated focused VSTest verification rebuilt the mod and tests with .NET SDK
10.0.401 and VINTAGE_STORY=G:\Vintagestory: 1,839 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped.
No warnings were reported in target-geometry/cache or presentation files;
unrelated existing warnings remain. The second direct correction review and fresh
independent audit-stage-completion audit found Phase 2 fully satisfied with no
required findings or evidence gaps. This includes the user-approved
texture-independent coverage correction: no bitmap acquisition/scanning,
alpha-based clipping/rejection, or required material-alpha shader sampling.
All four static material passes and inherited per-face passes are tested;
liquid/extra-transform paths were omitted by the historical Phase 2 source gate.
Those source restrictions and class qualifications are superseded by the user's
Phase 4 native target-instance acquisition correction below.
Support limits, installed 1.22.7 engine-source evidence, identity, transforms,
ownership, and invalidation hooks are documented in
[BuildBrushArchitecture.md](BuildBrushArchitecture.md#snapping-target-geometry-and-owned-cache).
Actual lifecycle-event wiring remains Phase 4; live fidelity/readability and
performance qualification remain Phase 6. No drawing or shader support is claimed.

## Phase 3: Procedural shader and isolated drawing responsibility

Depends on: Phases 1 and 2.
Outcome: A buildable overlay drawing path consumes the presentation contract and
supported geometry. Live registration and shader compatibility are gated in Phase 4.

    ✔ Add custom shader assets using the repository's existing asset conventions.
        ✔ Supply target positions/indices, target transform, camera matrices,
          selected face/in-plane axes, independent applied directions, color,
          opacity, width, and appropriate surface-facing information.
        ✔ Preserve block-local coordinates through vertex transformation; project
          into the selected face plane in the fragment shader.
        ✔ Evaluate procedural directional masks on selected-facing surfaces and
          discard fragments outside markings; use derivative softening as appropriate.
        ✔ Anchor meaning to existing snapping rules, not texture UV layout,
          arbitrary shape bounds, camera-relative directions, or triangle edges.
        ✔ Keep face-normal offset without its own glyph and avoid per-block artwork.
    ✔ Implement the clearly named overlay drawing responsibility.
        ✔ Draw the target mesh a second time at the original world target, never
          at BuildBrushInstance's substituted placement destination.
        ✔ Keep depth testing enabled, disable overlay depth writes, and restore
          all changed render state even if drawing fails.
        ✔ Provide a candidate render stage/order and small depth bias for Phase 4/6
          validation; do not assume bias alone orders the native selection outline,
          brush preview, or transparent surfaces.
        ✔ Handle shader compilation/replacement failure explicitly through
          repository-standard diagnostics and safe resource ownership. Disable the
          failed overlay without disabling ordinary brush operation.
    ✔ Build the changed C# project and test parameter/drawing-state decisions where
      existing test seams permit; do not report a C# build as shader compilation proof.

Completion criteria:
    ✔ Shader sources and drawing code exist, C# compilation succeeds, and source/test
      evidence covers correct inputs, target transform, masking intent, and state
      restoration. Live shader compilation and visual qualification remain pending.

Verification: Delegated focused VSTest execution rebuilt production and tests:
2,021 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped. Both shader assets are copied to test output;
no affected-file compiler warnings were emitted. The second direct implementation
review and fresh independent audit-stage-completion audit found Phase 3 fully
satisfied. The isolated renderer, provisional bands, OIT order 0.52, depth bias,
state restoration, failure handling, and remaining runtime obligations are documented
in [BuildBrushArchitecture.md](BuildBrushArchitecture.md#isolated-snapping-shader-drawing).
No live GLSL compilation, client registration, visual ordering/readability,
occlusion, depth-bias acceptance, or performance qualification is claimed.

## Phase 4: Client-session integration and lifecycle verification

Depends on: Phases 1 through 3.
Outcome: The overlay runs with the local brush session, follows current resolved
targets, and safely clears/retires resources. It is ready for final regression checks.

    ✔ Compose and retire the client-owned overlay through BuildBrushSystem_Client
      and the existing local controller/session lifecycle.
        ✔ Keep the server preview entity renderer responsible only for the existing
          preview; add no network fields, preview synchronization, or new input.
        ✔ Feed matching snapshots and supported geometry into drawing; clear/replace
          stale presentation immediately when a newly observed target lacks its result.
        ✔ Keep markings attached to the original target as preview destinations move.
    ✔ Wire presentation clearing and resource invalidation/disposal distinctly.
        ✔ Clear on target loss, build-mode disable, unsupported held items, death,
          world departure, and any stale/mismatched/suppressed presentation.
          Invalid tangential placement remains visible in red.
        ✔ Refresh supported item/target changes without carrying prior outcomes.
          Snapping-mode changes may retain valid cached geometry.
        ✔ Invalidate relevant geometry/resources on geometry changes, resource
          reload, and shader reload; compile and replace shader resources safely.
        ✔ Release owned GPU resources and subscriptions on world departure and
          system disposal. Ensure other transitions leave no stale overlay or
          obsolete/leaked resources without indiscriminately discarding valid caches.
        ✔ Keep ordinary selection, placement, and preview usable after overlay
          compilation/drawing failure; surface failures through existing diagnostics.
    ✔ Verify integration with focused tests and a local runtime smoke check.
        ✔ Test rapid target/face/hit/mode/item changes and delayed/mismatched results.
        ✔ Test mode toggles, suspension/resumption, death, world exit, disposal,
          geometry changes, resource/shader reload, and shader failure.
        ✔ Check rebuild/upload counts and disposal/subscription ownership; ensure
          repeated retirement/replacement does not dispose borrowed resources.
        ✔ Compile shaders in the installed game runtime and record version and
          diagnostics; exercise reload/failure recovery without breaking the brush.
        ✔ Confirm the chosen render stage registers/deregisters correctly and
          leaves native render state intact; visual ordering still needs Phase 6.

Completion criteria:
    ✔ Focused integration/lifecycle tests pass, shaders compile in the intended
      runtime, failure leaves brush operation available, and no stale presentation
      or obsolete/leaked resources remain after the tested lifecycle transitions.

Implementation and verification history (before final runtime approval):
Client-session wiring and focused tests are implemented.
Delegated rebuilt verification passed 2,109 tests, 0 failed, 0 skipped; Release
publish succeeded with existing unrelated nullable/analyzer warnings only.
The installed engine file version is 1.22.7. The first live smoke failed: no
indicator appeared on a vertical slab despite accepted tangential snapping, and
the logs showed no snapping shader compilation attempt. The source gate rejected
the native slab class/rotation behaviors. The user's subsequent correction removes
the default-mesh whitelist entirely: acquisition now observes the original target's
actual native terrain tessellation, including block entities, variants, transforms,
and snow geometry. Pending/empty native output is not replaced with guessed shapes.
Delegated focused Release verification passed 1,050 tests, with 0 failed/skipped;
293 additional session/UI regressions also passed. Release publish succeeded.
This includes installation of the capture observers against installed 1.22.7
engine signatures, all close-range LOD pools, decor parity, and the native
MarkBlockDirty/BlockChanged feedback guard. Independent review findings were fixed
and affected-requirements rechecks are clean. The refactored candidate still
needs installed-game verification. The subsequent live smoke compiled the shader
successfully but failed visibility/depth qualification: top slabs were absent,
full blocks appeared only with axis-aligned offsets, and visible bands z-fought.
The correction removes redundant CPU surface rejection, retains native packed
normals/Z offsets and pass-specific depth conventions, and adds restored
slope-aware polygon offset. Updated automated verification does not substitute
for repeating those failed installed-game cases. Reload/failure recovery and
native-state smoke evidence also remain outstanding.
The corrected candidate passed 1,361 rebuilt managed tests, 0 failed/skipped,
and Release publish. A real, isolated OpenGL 4.6/RTX 4090 smoke compiled the shader
assets and exercised production render-state capture/apply/restore across 1,728
coverage/foreground-occlusion/depth-preservation/state-restoration cases, including
perspective and grazing views, native/fallback normals, all nine pass IDs, two
surface heights, and axis/diagonal directions. This does not qualify the failed
installed-game scene or complete Phase 4.
The next live smoke still showed the target texture cutting through cyan bands;
installed DLL/shader identity matched the published candidate. Investigation found
a native upload binding mismatch: the stripped position/flags mesh puts flags at
attribute 1, but the shader read attribute 3. The shader now reads attribute 1,
with regression checks coupling the captured attribute layout to the shader.
The earlier isolated harness manually bound attribute 3 and therefore did not
validate this production upload path. Its results are not evidence against this bug.
Rebuilt managed verification passed all 1,361 tests and Release publish.
The replacement real-GPU smoke uses actual native UploadMesh/RenderMesh, the
production capture/state/shaders, and native opaque vertex shader for terrain:
384 coverage/occlusion/depth-preservation cases passed. Switching only the flags
slot back to 3 reproduced 48 failures, all corrected at slot 1 without bias changes.
Installed-game retesting remains pending; Phase 4 is still incomplete.
The user subsequently confirmed the slot correction fixed z-fighting, but reported
camera-dependent on/off strobing during smoothed head-bob decay. The exact-hit
presentation identity gate rejected otherwise unchanged snapping directions between
placement updates. Render matching now compares permitted U/V regions using shared
snapping classification, retains strict target/face/box/mode/revision/source guards,
and preserves exact-hit matching in the placement update shortcut. The existing
validity refresh retains equivalent directions after its one fresh-hit rule check.
Ordinary zero-tangent results retain strict validity-refresh matching as well,
preserving their subsequent placement validation counts.
No extra placement resolution, validation, smoothing delay, or hysteresis is added.
New tests reproduced ten failures before the fix and exercise decaying camera/hit
motion, all faces, disabled axes, genuine region transitions, validity refresh,
nonfinite hits, and unchanged upload/validation counts.
The final stability candidate passed 1,378 rebuilt tests, 0 failed/skipped, and
Release publish, including ordinary-placement validation-count preservation.
Installed-game confirmation of the camera-strobing correction remains pending.
The user's subsequent clarification supersedes the separate strict/render
matching policies and ordinary-result exception above: use one semantic match
for target block position, face, and snapping data across all consumers. Exact
hit coordinates and selection-box index alone are not memoization keys.
Block identity, source eligibility, input revision, and mode invalidation remain
guards. Tests now require the same matcher to accept within-region motion and
ordinary-result reuse, without the prior exact-hit revalidation exception.
The second direct implementation review is complete. At that point all Phase 4
markers remained unchecked because the independent audit retained the runtime
evidence gap, subsequently closed by the user's approval below.
See [BuildBrushArchitecture.md](BuildBrushArchitecture.md#snapping-overlay-client-session-lifecycle)
for ownership, invalidation, diagnostics, and test coverage.

Final verification and acceptance, 2026-10-29:
The subsequent invalid-placement correction retains resolved tangential markings
in red, with valid markings cyan; 1,092 focused tests passed and Release publish
succeeded. The dedicated BrushSnappingShader now subclasses the game's ShaderProgram
and exposes all nine uniforms through typed property setters. Its 395 focused
shader/renderer/binding tests passed, including exact native setter mappings,
complete uniform coverage, lifecycle/failure recovery, and property-only renderer
access; Release publish succeeded.
After the remaining runtime checks were listed, the user confirmed
"yes its all fixed, approved." This accepts the Phase 4 installed-game smoke,
including camera/head-bob stability, red/cyan validity feedback, native shader
loading, lifecycle transitions, reload/failure recovery, and intact brush operation.
The installed engine version was previously identified as 1.22.7. This is
user-reported runtime acceptance, not a new agent-run diagnostic capture or
performance measurement. Phase 4 is complete; Phases 5 and 6 remain open for
distributable verification and comprehensive geometry/appearance/cost qualification.

