## Phase 6: User-run geometry, appearance, and performance qualification

Depends on: Phase 5 evaluation candidate and user access to an in-game session.
Outcome: The initial support set and presentation are accepted on live evidence,
or unresolved deficiencies remain explicitly open rather than declared complete.

    ☐ Have the user evaluate concrete glyph/color/width/opacity candidates.
        ☐ Verify all six faces, opposite-face signs, top/bottom dual horizontal
          directions, horizontal-only, vertical-only, combined, and None modes.
        ☐ Cross both center thresholds, including centered/disabled axes and
          two-axis corner situations, while changing camera orientation.
        ☐ Verify explicit W offset does not mislabel tangential movement and
          W-only/ordinary fallback never shows U/V marking, while invalid U/V
          snapping remains visible in red rather than successful cyan.
    ☐ Qualify the initial geometry set in the packaged runtime.
        ☐ Compare candidate cube/non-cubic meshes and transforms with actual world
          instances, not the placement preview or inventory appearance.
        ☐ Check narrow/recessed surfaces, readability, selected-facing coverage,
          and lack of misleading shape-boundary cues; omit failed targets/categories.
        ☐ Verify missing/empty native geometry omits the overlay without affecting
          selection, placement, or normal preview behavior, with no class blacklist.
        ☐ Record qualified examples and excluded categories with reasons;
          any support expansion must meet the same fidelity and readability checks.
    ☐ Validate rendering interaction and lifecycle with live evidence.
        ☐ Move the camera, target face, and preview destination independently;
          verify target anchoring, no stale markings, no surface fighting/flicker,
          and occlusion by intervening blocks.
        ☐ Check native selection outline, preview readability, transparent-scene
          interactions, surface-facing filtering, render order, and depth bias.
        ☐ Exercise build/snapping-mode transitions, supported/unsupported items,
          death, world departure/re-entry, resource reload, and shader failure.
    ☐ Measure rendering cost and confirm the cache behavior in the live runtime.
        ☐ Record comparable overlay-on/off timings, scene, hardware/runtime, and
          sampling method; do not substitute unit tests for GPU/frame evidence.
        ☐ Confirm repeated face/hit-region/mode changes do not rebuild geometry
          and required invalidation does rebuild on the appropriate thread.
        ☐ Obtain the user's explicit appearance/readability/performance decision.
          Fix failed criteria and repeat affected checks before accepting the feature.
    ☐ Finalize documentation and remove temporary qualification scaffolding.
        ☐ Record user verdicts, measured results, final support boundary, and any
          still-open checks in the implementation evidence; do not invent approval.
        ☐ Remove temporary forced failures, diagnostic rendering, and candidate-only
          hooks not needed by the supported feature; retain standard error reporting.
        ☐ Re-run affected focused checks/package validation after final adjustments.
        ☐ Update the README, architecture notes, proposal implementation status,
          and existing project.todo indicator item only to the extent proven complete.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ User evidence establishes appearance, all-face direction/readability,
      supported shape fidelity, overlay interaction, lifecycle behavior, and measured
      rendering cost for the final packaged candidate.
    ☐ Any deliberately excluded geometry is documented; unresolved required
      qualification prevents final acceptance, rather than becoming an implicit waiver.

## Final acceptance

    ☐ Every proposal requirement maps to implemented behavior and evidence;
      exclusions/deferred optional extensions have explicit rationale.
    ☐ All six faces and existing snapping/placement rules are preserved, with no
      tangential indication for ordinary fallback, None, or W-only; invalid
      tangential snapping is red and valid tangential snapping is cyan.
    ☐ Overlay geometry remains on the original target, supported shapes are
      qualified, and unsupported targets omit it without placement/selection changes.
